{
  "global_card_id": "external_liquidity",
  "global_canonical": "External Liquidity",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "ICT",
  "aliases": [
    "external liquidity sweep",
    "liquidity outside the range"
  ],
  "definition": "External liquidity refers to resting orders—primarily stop-losses—that accumulate beyond established swing highs and swing lows, outside the current price range. Smart money sweeps these external levels to trigger and absorb liquidity before reversing back into the range or continuing the trend. The sweep functions as a manipulation phase preceding the true directional move, distinguishing it from genuine breakouts.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "Daily Profiles": "Liquidity resting outside the current price range, beyond established swing highs or lows, where stop-loss orders accumulate. Price sweeps these external levels—triggering resting orders—then reverses back into the range rather than expanding directionally. Functions as a manipulation phase (Power of Three) before the true directional move, typically during high-probability session windows.",
    "SMC-Indicator-Supplement": "The swing highs and swing lows of a rally wave, representing price levels where stop-loss orders accumulate. Smart money targets these zones to trigger stops, absorbing liquidity for momentum before the next move."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "Mark the current price range or rally wave by identifying its swing high (Range High) and swing low (Range Low) on the working time frame.",
    "Locate resting liquidity beyond those boundaries: buy-side liquidity above the swing high (equal highs, prior swing highs, Previous Day High) and sell-side liquidity below the swing low (equal lows, prior swing lows, Previous Day Low).",
    "Confirm an external liquidity sweep when price extends beyond the swing high or low, triggers those stop orders, and then reverses—Daily Profiles emphasizes price closing back inside the range rather than sustaining the breakout on a higher time frame, while SMC-Indicator-Supplement notes price may either reverse or continue the trend after the sweep.",
    "Note directional context: a sweep on the opposite side of the prevailing order flow (e.g., sweeping sell-side liquidity in a bullish context) signals manipulation before continuation (Daily Profiles emphasis).",
    "Look for the sweep to occur during a high-probability session window (London 02:00–05:00 or New York AM 08:30–09:30 EST) to confirm a session-driven manipulation move (Daily Profiles emphasis)."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Treating the external liquidity sweep as a genuine breakout and entering in the direction of the sweep, rather than waiting for price to reverse back into the range.",
    "Ignoring the return-to-range dynamic and holding a position in the direction of the sweep instead of anticipating reversion to internal liquidity targets.",
    "Failing to distinguish external liquidity (beyond swing highs/lows) from internal liquidity (fair value gaps or equal highs/lows inside the range), leading to premature entries before the sweep is complete."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "A sweep of external liquidity signals the manipulation phase of the Power of Three model and marks a potential reversal zone after stops are absorbed. In Daily Profiles, particularly a Seek and Destroy profile, the sweep acts as a magnet-release driving price back into the range; after the purge, traders look for a Change in the State of Delivery paired with an order block or fair value gap, targeting internal levels (equilibrium, daily open) or the opposite side of the range. SMC-Indicator-Supplement similarly advises waiting for the sweep of a swing high or low, then entering at points of interest like fair value gaps or order blocks—but allows for either reversion toward internal liquidity or trend continuation with new momentum.",
  "merge_notes": "Key divergence on post-sweep direction: Daily Profiles frames external liquidity sweeps as a manipulation phase that primarily reverses price back into the range (Power of Three, session-window context), whereas SMC-Indicator-Supplement defines it more simply around rally-wave swing extremes and treats the sweep as enabling either reversal or trend continuation. Session-timing and order-flow-context rules are unique to Daily Profiles; SMC-Indicator-Supplement provided no common mistakes.",
